<template>
  <section class="discord-guide">
    <div class="discord-hero"><div><span class="eyebrow">RUNBACK × DISCORD</span><h1>Call the next match.<span> Automatically.</span></h1><p>Runback can announce station calls, mention linked players, and accept safe tournament commands in Discord.</p></div><div class="discord-stat"><b>{{ linked }}</b><small>PLAYERS LINKED<br>OF {{ entrants }} ENTRANTS</small></div></div>
    <div class="discord-feature-grid"><article><b>01</b><h2>Automatic callouts</h2><p>Assign a station in Runback. The bot posts the matchup, station and set length, then mentions only entrants linked to that match.</p></article><article><b>02</b><h2>Staff commands</h2><p>Check the queue, standings and schedule; call players; record results; and mark yourself checked in.</p></article><article><b>03</b><h2>Local by design</h2><p>The bridge listens on your computer. The Discord token stays in the bot’s local .env file and never enters Runback’s dashboard.</p></article></div>
    <div class="reference-note"><b>This needs one setup step outside the dashboard.</b> Create your own Discord application and bot. No messages are sent until you add its token, server/channel IDs and a shared bridge secret, then start the companion bot.</div>
    <section class="org-card discord-setup-card"><span class="eyebrow">FIRST / CREATE YOUR DISCORD APP</span><ol><li>Open the <a href="https://discord.com/developers/applications" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Discord Developer Portal ↗</a> and create an application. Add a bot on its Bot page and copy its token once.</li><li>Use OAuth2 → URL Generator with the <b>bot</b> and <b>applications.commands</b> scopes. Give it View Channel, Send Messages and Embed Links in your event channel. Open the generated URL and add it to your server.</li><li>Copy the application ID, server ID and the ID of the channel where calls should appear. Discord Developer Mode exposes Copy ID in the context menu.</li></ol></section>
    <section class="org-card discord-setup-card"><span class="eyebrow">SECOND / CONNECT THE LOCAL BRIDGE</span><p>Runback's installer uses an older Electron runtime, so the Discord companion runs separately with Node.js 24.17 or newer. It connects only to <code>127.0.0.1</code>.</p><p>In the NodeCG root (the folder containing <code>index.js</code> and <code>bundles</code>), create a <code>cfg</code> folder if one is missing, then create <code>cfg/runback.json</code>. In an installed app this is under <code>resources/lib/nodecg/cfg</code>. Add this field (merge it into existing JSON and preserve the other settings):</p><pre>{
  "discordApiSecret": "PASTE_THE_SAME_RANDOM_SECRET_HERE"
}</pre><p>Make a 32-byte random secret, for example in PowerShell:</p><pre>$bytes = New-Object byte[] 32
    $rng = [Security.Cryptography.RandomNumberGenerator]::Create()
    try { $rng.GetBytes($bytes) } finally { $rng.Dispose() }
$secret = [BitConverter]::ToString($bytes).Replace('-', '').ToLowerInvariant()
$secret</pre><p>Use that same 64-character value as <code>RUNBACK_API_SECRET</code> in <code>discord-bot/.env</code>. Treat both the bot token and this secret like passwords.</p></section>
    <section class="org-card discord-setup-card"><span class="eyebrow">THIRD / START THE BOT</span><ol><li>Open the included <code>discord-bot</code> folder. Copy <code>.env.example</code> to <code>.env</code> and fill in the bot token, application ID, server ID, channel ID and shared secret.</li><li>Set <code>RUNBACK_API_URL</code> to <code>http://127.0.0.1:9090/runback-discord/api/v1</code>. If your NodeCG server uses another port, replace <code>9090</code> with that port.</li><li>Start Runback first. In PowerShell opened to <code>discord-bot</code>, run <code>npm.cmd install</code>, then <code>npm.cmd start</code>. Keep this window open during the tournament. The bot registers slash commands when it starts.</li></ol><div class="reference-note">Start this bot before calling matches. Callouts are triggered when a match is assigned to a station. Existing station matches at startup are not broadcast automatically; use <code>/runback call</code> to resend one.</div></section>
    <section class="org-card"><div class="section-title"><h2>Link players before the bracket</h2><span>{{ linked }} linked</span></div><p>Players can run <code>/runback link</code> and pick their entrant themselves. Or import a CSV with a fourth <code>discord_id</code> column. Discord IDs are digits, not usernames or invite links. A player without a linked account still appears in callout text but won’t receive a personal ping.</p><pre>name,team,country,discord_id
Player One,Team A,PK,123456789012345678</pre><p>Discord calls ping the two players only; the bot suppresses other role and everyone mentions.</p></section>
    <section class="org-card"><h2>Discord commands</h2><div class="discord-command-list"><code>/runback status</code><span>Event, checked-in count, active sets and champion</span><code>/runback matches</code><span>Ready and playing sets</span><code>/runback schedule</code><span>Upcoming event blocks</span><code>/runback standings</code><span>Placements and set records</span><code>/runback link</code><span>Link your Discord account to your entrant</span><code>/runback checkin</code><span>Check yourself in before bracket lock</span><code>/runback call</code><span>Staff resends a player call</span><code>/runback result</code><span>Authorized staff reports a score</span><code>/runback help</code><span>Command guide</span></div><p>Result reporting and manual calls require a server administrator or one of the staff roles you add to <code>RUNBACK_ADMIN_ROLE_IDS</code>. Checking in and linking are limited to your own account.</p></section>
    <p class="muted">Keep the computer awake and connected. If the bot or Runback stops, automatic calls pause. A channel send failure appears in the bot terminal; staff can retry with <code>/runback call</code>. This integration does not send DMs or read channel history.</p>
